From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/dp: End the CRC capture when the CRTC is off

drm_dp_aux_crc_work() reads the sink once per frame until userspace
closes the CRC file, and starts each pass with drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank().
While the CRTC is off that call warns with a backtrace and returns at
once, because drm_crtc_vblank_off() makes drm_vblank_get() fail. With
the file open, turning the CRTC off therefore makes the worker warn on
every pass until the file is closed. Every user of drm_dp_start_crc()
has this, for example the Analogix DP bridge behind the Rockchip VOP and
amdgpu, which call drm_crtc_vblank_off() when they disable the CRTC.
drm_dp_stop_crc() cannot end the capture on a disable: it waits for the
worker, which only ends when the file is closed.

Take a vblank reference before each wait and end the loop when it is
refused, which it is while the CRTC is off. The frame CRC ABI asks
userspace to set a capture up again after a modeset, so the file stays
open without a worker, and a capture that ended is not resumed when the
CRTC is turned on again. Closing the file still calls drm_dp_stop_crc(),
whose flush returns at once, and stops the sink.

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S (Allwinner A733) with a DisplayPort monitor:
with the CRC data file of the CRTC open, blanking the framebuffer turned
the CRTC off, and drm_dp_aux_crc_work() warned "vblank not available"
from drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank() on every pass.

Not covered: a pass that is already waiting for a vblank when the CRTC
is turned off keeps waiting. Where the vblank counter does not advance
at drm_crtc_vblank_off(), as without a hardware counter or timestamps,
the wait runs into its timeout, which warns once, and the loop ends at
the next pass. A CRTC that is turned off and on again within one wait is
not noticed, and the capture goes on. A capture that userspace relies on
after a modeset is set up again by closing the file and opening it.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on one board with a DisplayPort
monitor; in repeated cycles of blanking the framebuffer with the CRC
file open, the capture ended with the CRTC, the kernel logged at most
one vblank timeout warning per cycle and no "vblank not available", and
closing the file returned at once.

Fixes: 79c1da7c3bf7 ("drm/dp: add helpers for capture of frame CRCs")
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c
index f9e65bd266cf..a99f548e9e68 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c
@@ -2298,7 +2298,14 @@ static void drm_dp_aux_crc_work(struct work_struct *work)
 
 	crtc = aux->crtc;
 	while (crtc->crc.opened) {
+		/*
+		 * A CRTC that is off has no vblank to wait for: the capture
+		 * ends, and userspace sets it up again after the modeset.
+		 */
+		if (drm_crtc_vblank_get(crtc))
+			break;
 		drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank(crtc);
+		drm_crtc_vblank_put(crtc);
 		if (!crtc->crc.opened)
 			break;
 
@@ -2473,6 +2480,12 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(drm_dp_psr_setup_time);
  * @aux: DisplayPort AUX channel
  * @crtc: CRTC displaying the frames whose CRCs are to be captured
  *
+ * The capture ends at the first pass that finds the vblank of @crtc off, for
+ * example after the CRTC was turned off, and it is not resumed: the frame CRC
+ * ABI asks userspace to set a capture up again after a modeset. A CRTC that
+ * is turned off and on again within one pass may keep the capture going.
+ * drm_dp_stop_crc() has to be called all the same, to stop the sink.
+ *
  * Returns 0 on success or a negative error code on failure.
  */
 int drm_dp_start_crc(struct drm_dp_aux *aux, struct drm_crtc *crtc)
